<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Modernism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Modernism was an artistic and cultural movement of the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries that broke with traditional forms. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>'s profile, the Benin bronzes looted in 1897 influenced European modernism through the admiration of artists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Picasso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-matisse">Matisse</a>. African art more broadly was an important source for modernist artists.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Modernism</b>, with origins as early as the 1880s, had already made itself felt in Germany before the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>It took in a Pan-African perspective, “high” and “low” culture, spirituals, blues and jazz, and traditional as well as experimental forms such as <b>modernism</b> and jazz poetry.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ottoman Empire and the Middle East liberalism influenced the Tanzimat and Al-Nahda reforms, the Young Ottomans and Islamic <b>Modernism</b>, though its books and papers reached mainly intellectuals and the emerging middle class.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
